      teen: 'This is one of the shortest verses in the Bible and one of the most useful. "Can two walk together, except they be agreed?" (Amos 3:3). That is a question, and the answer is no. But look at the ORDER, because that is the whole point. Agree first. Then walk. Not walk first and figure out the agreeing later. Most people do it backwards, and it feels completely reasonable at the time. You like somebody. You trust them. You are both excited about the same idea. So you start. And you think the details will work themselves out, because you get along. Here is what actually happens. The details do not work themselves out. They show up later as a fight. And the fight feels like betrayal, like the other person changed. Usually they did not change at all. You just never asked, so you filled in the blank with what YOU assumed, and they filled in the same blank with what THEY assumed, and both of you thought it was settled. So here is the practical version, and you can use it on anything — a business, a group project, a band, a trip. Before you start, write down what you think the answers are. What is this for. Who decides. What happens to the money. How does somebody leave if they want out. Then have the other person write their answers WITHOUT seeing yours. Then compare. Wherever the two pages are different, that is a disagreement you already had. You did not create it by asking. You just found it early, while it is still cheap. And here is the part that takes some courage. Sometimes you compare the pages and discover you do not agree at all. That is not the conversation failing. That is the conversation WORKING. It is far better to find that out over a piece of paper than two years in with money in the middle of it. One more thing, because this is where people get it backwards. Asking these questions does not mean you distrust somebody. It feels like it does. That is why almost nobody asks them. You are excited, you like each other, and bringing up who decides what feels like you are accusing them of something. But think about what you are actually doing. You are making sure that in three years you are both still describing the same arrangement. That is not distrust. That is the opposite — it is taking the thing seriously enough to want it to survive. The people who skip it are not being more trusting. They are just being less careful, and the bill for that arrives years later with interest.',
