      teen: 'Before the Bible warns you about bad partners, it makes the case FOR having them. And notice what reason it gives, because it is not the one you would expect. "Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour" (Ecclesiastes 4:9). You might think the argument is going to be about getting more done. Two people, twice the work. It is not. Read the next verse. "For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up" (Ecclesiastes 4:10). The argument is about FALLING. Not what two people can build on a good day. What happens on the worst day. When one of them gets sick, or makes a bad call, or loses heart, or just cannot get up that morning. Alone, that is where everything stops. With somebody, it is a rough week. And then there is the line at the end that people quote without noticing what it is measuring. "a threefold cord is not quickly broken" (Ecclesiastes 4:12). A cord that is not broken QUICKLY. That is a strength-under-pulling measurement. Nobody talks about how hard a rope is to break unless something is pulling on it. So the whole passage assumes trouble is coming. It is not a nice thought about teamwork. It is a plan for pressure. Here is how to use it on your own life right now. Write down the things that only YOU can do. The password only you know. The person who only talks to you. The decision nobody else is allowed to make. Every single one of those is a place where you falling stops everything. Then write a name next to each one — who picks it up if you go down for two weeks? Any blank you cannot fill is not really a question about partnership. It is a weak spot with your name on it. And notice the other thing the passage says, which people quote and then do not think about. Two are better than one because they have a good reward for their labour. LABOUR. Not company. Not friendship. Work. This is not a passage about being lonely, it is a passage about doing something hard. So when you are thinking about who to build with, do not just ask who you enjoy. Ask who will still be there on the bad day, because that is the day the verse is actually about. And do the honest version too. Ask whether YOU would be. It is a much easier question to ask about somebody else.',
