        title: 'A Deal, Not a Yoke',
        body: 'Hassan did business with everybody and was sometimes criticised for it. He bought from a man whose politics he found repellent, used a contractor known for a colourful private life, and sold to whoever turned up with money. What he would not do, and this confused the same critics, was go into anything with any of them. His brother called it inconsistent. Hassan said it was the most consistent thing about him. A purchase ends: you pay, you take the thing, and if you never see the man again nothing of yours is left in his hands. A partnership does not end, not cleanly, not without his cooperation or a court. He said he was perfectly happy to transact with a man he would not want deciding things on his behalf, and that most people had it exactly backwards — they were fussy about who they bought a van from and casual about whose signature could bind them. He turned down three partnerships in his life and did about nine thousand transactions. He says the transactions have all been forgotten and every one of the three refusals is still, quietly, the reason he has a house.',
