    lesson: 'Paul reaches for a piece of farm equipment to make this point, and the equipment IS the argument. "Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?" (2 Corinthians 6:14). REBUILD THE IMAGE, because it is routinely lost in the quoting. A yoke is a beam laid across the shoulders of two draught animals, binding both of them to one implement. Its entire function is the elimination of independent movement. Neither animal sets its own pace. Neither chooses its own line. Neither stops when it would rather stop. That is not a flaw in the design — it is the whole point of the design, and it is what makes a plough cut a straight furrow instead of wandering. NOW TAKE THE WORD "UNEQUALLY" SERIOUSLY, because almost everyone assumes it means the weaker animal gets carried along. It does not. In a mismatched yoke the stronger beast is restrained for the entire working day, and the weaker one is dragged, chafed and worn raw. The damage runs in BOTH directions and it runs continuously. There is no quantity of goodwill, patience or mutual affection that makes an unequal yoke comfortable for either party, because the discomfort is structural rather than relational. AND PAUL IMMEDIATELY SPECIFIES WHICH MISMATCH HE MEANS, which matters, because it stops the verse being stretched into a general rule about avoiding people one finds difficult. "what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?" The mismatch in view is one of DIRECTION AND ALLEGIANCE — what a person is ultimately aimed at — not one of temperament, competence, speed or agreeableness. A slow partner is not an unequal yoke. A partner pointed somewhere you cannot go is. NOW THE DISTINCTION THAT MAKES THIS USABLE, and it is the one commercial language works hardest to obscure, because the word "partner" is applied to both instruments indiscriminately. A TRANSACTION is bounded. Defined scope, defined consideration, a clean end — and after the end, either party may simply decline the next one. You hire a roofer; he roofs; you pay; it is finished. A YOKE is structural. Shared liability. Commingled assets. Joint signatures. An entity, a guarantee, a co-signed note. In a yoke, the other party’s decision becomes your obligation, and your exit requires either his cooperation or a court. THE PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE FOLLOWS DIRECTLY and it is more permissive than people expect. You may transact broadly, and you should — with people of every persuasion, on ordinary commercial terms, and the Word does not restrict that. What deserves an entirely different order of scrutiny is the yoke, because the yoke is what converts another man’s judgment into your liability. SO THE QUESTION THAT TESTS ANY PROPOSED YOKE is not whether you like this person, or trust him today, or have had good dealings with him so far. Those are the wrong questions because they are all measured in the present tense. The question is: when this party moves in a direction I cannot follow — and over a long enough horizon, he will — what happens to me, and by what mechanism do I get out? A man who cannot answer that has not evaluated a partnership. He has only evaluated a friendship, and then signed something else.',
