    lesson: 'Scripture declines to tidy this episode up, and the refusal is the lesson. ESTABLISH THE STANDING OF THE TWO MEN FIRST, because it is what makes this uncomfortable rather than instructive-at-a-distance. Barnabas is the man who vouched for Paul when the Jerusalem church would not receive him — who staked his own credibility on a former persecutor. The two of them had carried the first missionary journey together and had faced genuine physical danger side by side. This is not a pairing of convenience. The presenting issue is a judgment call about personnel: whether to take Mark, who had withdrawn from the previous journey. AND THE TEXT STATES THE OUTCOME WITHOUT ANY MITIGATION WHATSOEVER. "And the contention was so sharp between them, that they departed asunder one from the other" (Acts 15:39). SO SHARP. The writer had every opportunity to soften that and did not take it. NOW THE CRITICAL OBSERVATION, and it has to be drawn out slowly, because readers expect a verdict and will supply one themselves if nobody stops them. THE NARRATIVE ASSIGNS NO FAULT. Neither man is rebuked. Neither is vindicated. There is no editorial comment, no later correction, no passage in which one of them recognises his error. What follows the split is simply a report of continued activity: "and so Barnabas took Mark, and sailed unto Cyprus; And Paul chose Silas, and departed, being recommended by the brethren unto the grace of God" (Acts 15:39-40). TWO CONCLUSIONS FOLLOW, AND THEY PULL IN DELIBERATELY OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS. THE FIRST IS PERMISSIVE, and it is needed in a course whose preceding lessons have been cautionary. Separation is not presented here as a failure of faith, of character or of love. Two competent, committed and genuinely godly parties reached an impasse on a judgment call and concluded they could not proceed jointly — and Scripture records this as a thing that happened rather than as a thing that went wrong. A partnership course that treats every dissolution as sin will produce operators who remain yoked long past the point of usefulness, tolerating arrangements that damage both parties and the work, because leaving has been made to feel like betrayal. That is its own kind of harm and this passage forecloses it. THE SECOND IS RESTRICTIVE, and it is where the actual discipline lives. Observe carefully what did NOT occur here. Neither party abandoned the work. Neither conducted a campaign against the other’s reputation. Neither attempted to deprive the other of resources, standing or co-workers. Neither spent the following years explaining to anyone who would listen how he had been wronged. Each man took a colleague and continued, and the capacity of the enterprise was not halved by the separation — it was DOUBLED. SO THE TRANSFERABLE STANDARD IS EXACTING, AND IT IS TESTABLE. The question is not whether a partnership ended. It is not even whether it ended badly; this one plainly did, and the text says so in the plainest available words. The question is whether both parties remained able to do the work afterwards, and whether either of them spent the following years litigating the past instead of building something. Notice that this standard says nothing at all about who was right — which is exactly the point the passage makes by declining to say it either.',
