    lesson: 'Six lessons in, a false binary has probably formed, and this is the point to name it. A course that spends its length on partnerships tends to leave an impression that the choice is between going it entirely alone and yoking yourself to somebody. Solomon offers a third instrument in a single line, and the value of it is in what the line leaves out. "Without counsel purposes are disappointed: but in the multitude of counsellors they are established" (Proverbs 15:22). ATTEND FIRST TO WHAT IS ABSENT, because the absences are the whole teaching. There is no equity in that sentence. No governance, no board seat, no vote. No liability, no joint signature, no claim of any kind on what the venture eventually becomes. A counsellor delivers the thing people most often form partnerships to obtain — a second mind, experience you do not personally have, the question you would never have thought to ask — and takes on none of the structural entanglement lesson three described. He does not become your obligation. You do not need his cooperation to leave. THEN ATTEND TO THE WORD "MULTITUDE", which readers routinely skip straight past. The proverb does not commend A counsellor. It commends several, and the reason is practical rather than decorative. One adviser gives you exactly one worldview, and from inside a single conversation you have no way at all of distinguishing that man’s considered judgment from his temperament, his last bad experience, or the way his own business happens to be structured. Four give you a distribution. Where they converge, you are probably looking at something real about the world. Where they scatter badly, you have learned that the matter is genuinely uncertain — and that finding is worth considerably more than a confident answer that happened to be wrong. NOW MAKE IT OPERATIONAL, because this is the cheapest instrument in the entire course and by some distance the least used. Write down the decisions actually in front of you. For each one, identify three or four people who have DONE that specific thing — not friends who will be encouraging, and not people who will enjoy the conversation, but practitioners who will be accurate. Ask a narrow question rather than a broad one, because a broad question returns a broad answer and costs exactly the same hour. And pay where payment is appropriate: an hour of a good surveyor, an accountant or a lawyer is a rounding error set against the cost of a partnership entered into mainly because you wanted somebody to think alongside you. AND CLOSE WITH THE HONEST LIMIT, because this must not become an argument against partnership, which lesson two spent its whole length defending. Counsel cannot supply capital. It cannot carry the work. It cannot lift you up when you fall, which is precisely the case Ecclesiastes made for having a partner at all. What counsel supplies is JUDGMENT. So the test is diagnostic rather than moral: work out what you are actually short of. If it is money you cannot otherwise raise, or hands beside you, or the resilience of a second cord, then a partnership is the right instrument and this course has spent six lessons telling you how to enter one carefully. But if what you are short of is judgment — and it very often is — then a yoke is an extraordinarily expensive way to buy an hour of somebody’s attention.',
