    lesson: 'The course ends on the one thing no document produces. SET THE SITUATION FIRST, because the sentence only lands once the difficulty is real. Nehemiah is rebuilding the wall under external threat — ridicule at first, then organised conspiracy — and under internal strain that the text does not minimise: exhaustion, an overwhelming quantity of rubble, and a judgment among the workers themselves that the job is beyond them. Then the account gives its result and its cause in a single sentence. "So built we the wall; and all the wall was joined together unto the half thereof: for the people had a mind to work" (Nehemiah 4:6). READ THE CONJUNCTION, because everything hangs on it. FOR. That is a causal claim, and it names none of the things a modern reader reaches for. Not superior planning. Not the withdrawal of the enemy. Not favourable terms, better resources, or leadership technique — and Nehemiah demonstrably had the last of those in quantity. The cause given is a DISPOSITION IN THE PEOPLE — which is where Yahweh so often locates the outcome, in what is in a person rather than in what is arranged around him. NOW DRAW THE CONSEQUENCE CAREFULLY, because this can be misheard as an argument against everything the preceding seven lessons taught. It is not, and it is worth saying so explicitly. Agreement precedes the walk. A yoke is structurally different from a transaction and deserves a different order of scrutiny. Exit terms are negotiated at formation, behind the veil, while both parties can still afford to be generous. Counsel can be had without entanglement. Every one of those prevents a specific, common and expensive failure, and skipping them is not faith — it is carelessness with other people’s money and usually with a friendship as well. WHAT THIS LESSON ADDS IS THAT ALL SEVEN ARE NECESSARY AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS SUFFICIENT. Consider what a perfect agreement actually guarantees when the disposition is absent. It guarantees that when the thing fails, the failure will be orderly. Rights will be preserved. The exit will execute as drafted. Everyone will be able to demonstrate that they behaved correctly. And the wall will not be built. A well-drafted partnership between two parties who do not genuinely want the thing finished produces a well-documented disappointment, on schedule, with nobody at fault. SO THE FINAL QUESTION IN PARTNER SELECTION IS NOT A LEGAL ONE, and it cannot be answered in an interview, because everybody interviews as though they have a mind to work. It is answered by OBSERVATION, over time, in small things. Does this person arrive. Does he carry his portion on the days nobody is measuring it. Does he want the thing DONE, or does he want to be IN it — which are different desires that look identical for about the first six months. What has he finished before, and what did finishing it cost him. WHY THIS LESSON COMES LAST, and it is the same reason every course in this department ends where it does. The seven before it are technique, and technique is what you apply to other people. This one turns round. Ask the question of yourself, with the same accuracy you applied to them, and answer it honestly — because a man assessing partners for a mind to work who does not have one himself is simply going to keep finding that everybody he meets is a disappointment.',
